# 115 · A judge-only record run keeps what it measured, and ends when it is recorded

**Status:** `PROPOSED` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011)

## What

A plan only; nothing of it is built.

A campaign often needs a record run first: the judge measures the starting point once, with no agents, and the next campaign's kit uses what it recorded (reference outputs to compare candidates against, a baseline to report). Today:

- **Only a few numbers survive a judge run.** A cell keeps about 2,000 characters of its reason and up to 24 numbers. A reference of a few kilobytes has to be cut into slices of base64 across several cells, and a file can't be kept at all.
- **A record run never ends as recorded.** It stays running until someone closes it.

So a judge run should keep a cell's files the kit names (small, size-capped, with their SHA-256 in the record), give them to the owner and to a later kit commit, and a judge-only run whose cells are all measured should end with a reason that says so.

## Why

On 2026-10-23, the draft of a speed hill climb needed a record run to store the starting point's reference outputs for a numerics guard. The draft had to carry the reference as five base64 slices in cell reasons, and its record run would have stayed open after its one measurement.

## Done when

- [ ] Written when the goal is accepted into a version.

## Decisions

None yet.

## Open questions

- The size limit of a kept file, and where it lives: the rig's ledger, its exports, or control.
- How a later kit commit takes a kept file: by hand, or through a command.

## Progress

- 2026-10-23: Goal written as a plan, from the draft of a speed hill climb.

